## 1.  What is a **delegate**?

In C#, a delegate is a **type‑safe reference** to a method (or a group of methods).  
Think of it as a *function pointer* in C/C++ but with strong typing and safety guarantees.

```csharp
// 1.1. Declaring a delegate type
delegate void MyDelegate(string message);

// 1.2. Creating an instance of that delegate
void SayHello(string msg) { Console.WriteLine(msg); }
MyDelegate d = SayHello;          // method group conversion

// 1.3. Invoking the delegate
d("Hello, world!");               // prints: Hello, world!
```

### Key points

| Feature | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Strong typing** | The delegate type enforces the signature (return type + parameters). |
| **Multicast** | A delegate can reference *multiple* methods; invoking it calls them all in order. |
| **First‑class** | Delegates are objects; you can pass them around, store them, etc. |

---

## 2.  What is an **event**?

An event is a *special* delegate that adds a layer of encapsulation.  
It lets other classes **subscribe** (attach handlers) and **unsubscribe** (detach), but only the declaring class can **raise** the event.

```csharp
// 2.1. Declaring an event inside a class
class Alarm
{
    // The delegate type that the event will use
    public delegate void AlarmEventHandler(object sender, AlarmEventArgs e);

    // The event itself
    public event AlarmEventHandler AlarmTriggered;

    // A helper method that raises the event
    protected virtual void OnAlarmTriggered(AlarmEventArgs e)
    {
        // Raise only if there is at least one subscriber
        AlarmTriggered?.Invoke(this, e);
    }

    // Simulate the alarm going off
    public void Trigger(string location)
    {
        OnAlarmTriggered(new AlarmEventArgs { Location = location, Time = DateTime.Now });
    }
}

// Custom EventArgs
class AlarmEventArgs : EventArgs
{
    public string Location { get; set; }
    public DateTime Time { get; set; }
}
```

### Why use an event instead of a public delegate?

| Aspect | Delegate field | Event |
|--------|----------------|-------|
| **Encapsulation** | Any code can assign a new delegate (overwrite subscribers). | Only the declaring class can raise it; others can only add/remove handlers. |
| **Safety** | `delegate` can be set to `null` or replaced inadvertently. | The `+=` / `-=` syntax ensures handlers are *added* or *removed* cleanly. |
| **Convention** | Less explicit about publish/subscribe pattern. | Communicates intent clearly: *this class publishes* `SomeEvent`. |

---

## 3.  Simple example – “Alarm” and “Listener”

Let’s put it all together in a **self‑contained console application**.

```csharp
using System;

// 3.1. Event arguments
class AlarmEventArgs : EventArgs
{
    public string Location { get; set; }
    public DateTime Time { get; set; }
}

// 3.2. The publisher (the one that raises the event)
class Alarm
{
    // Delegate type for the event (could also use EventHandler<AlarmEventArgs>)
    public delegate void AlarmEventHandler(object sender, AlarmEventArgs e);

    // Event that subscribers can hook into
    public event AlarmEventHandler AlarmTriggered;

    // Raise the event
    protected virtual void OnAlarmTriggered(AlarmEventArgs e)
    {
        AlarmTriggered?.Invoke(this, e);  // Safe invoke (null‑conditional)
    }

    // Simulate the alarm firing
    public void Trigger(string location)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[Alarm] Triggering at {location}");
        OnAlarmTriggered(new AlarmEventArgs { Location = location, Time = DateTime.Now });
    }
}

// 3.3. The subscriber (the one that handles the event)
class AlarmListener
{
    public void Subscribe(Alarm alarm)
    {
        // Attach a handler
        alarm.AlarmTriggered += HandleAlarm;
    }

    public void Unsubscribe(Alarm alarm)
    {
        alarm.AlarmTriggered -= HandleAlarm;
    }

    // The actual handler that matches the delegate signature
    private void HandleAlarm(object sender, AlarmEventArgs e)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[Listener] Alarm received at {e.Location} ({e.Time:HH:mm:ss})");
    }
}

// 3.4. Demo in Main
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Alarm alarm = new Alarm();
        AlarmListener listener = new AlarmListener();

        // Subscribe
        listener.Subscribe(alarm);

        // Trigger the alarm
        alarm.Trigger("Kitchen");

        // Unsubscribe (optional)
        listener.Unsubscribe(alarm);

        // Trigger again – listener won't see it
        alarm.Trigger("Bedroom");
    }
}
```

### What happens when you run it?

```
[Alarm] Triggering at Kitchen
[Listener] Alarm received at Kitchen (12:34:56)
[Alarm] Triggering at Bedroom
```

Notice that after unsubscribing, the second trigger no longer produces a listener message.

