## 4.  Bonus: Using the built‑in `EventHandler<T>` pattern

Instead of defining your own delegate type, you can use the generic `EventHandler<T>` delegate, which is a standard in .NET.

```csharp
public class Alarm
{
    public event EventHandler<AlarmEventArgs> AlarmTriggered;

    protected virtual void OnAlarmTriggered(AlarmEventArgs e)
        => AlarmTriggered?.Invoke(this, e);
}
```

The rest of the code stays exactly the same. Using `EventHandler<T>` improves interoperability with many frameworks and libraries.

---

## 5.  Quick recap

| Concept | Purpose | Typical usage |
|---------|---------|---------------|
| **Delegate** | Type‑safe method reference (can be multicast). | Callback functions, LINQ `Select`, async continuations. |
| **Event** | Encapsulated multicast delegate for publish/subscribe. | UI button clicks, timer ticks, custom notifications. |

Delegates and events are the backbone of the observer pattern in C#, enabling clean, decoupled communication between objects.  By following the pattern shown above, you can create reusable, maintainable code that reacts to changes or actions in other parts of your application.
